ardship seriously."}]}]},"subsequently":{"h":"subsequently","i":"/ˈsʌb.sɪ.kwənt.li/","a":"en-us-subsequently.ogg","s":[{"p":"adverb","d":[{"m":"If something happens subsequently, it happens after something else in time.","e":"He studied law and subsequently became a judge."}]}]},"thrasher":{"h":"thrasher","i":"/ˈθræʃɚ/","s":[{"p":"noun","d":[{"m":"(countable) A thrasher is a person or machine that separates grain from plants by beating them.","e":"The farmer used a thrasher to process the wheat."},{"m":"(countable) A thrasher is a type of bird with a long tail and a curved beak, known for digging in the ground.","e":"We saw a brown thrasher searching for food in the garden."}]}]},"loathes":{"h":"loathes","l":"loathe","q":"verb"},"loathed":{"h":"loathed","l":"loathe","q":"verb"},"loathing":{"h":"loathing","l":"loathe","q":"verb"},"neurons":{"h":"neurons","l":"neuron","q":"noun"},"thrashers":{"h":"thrashers","l":"thrasher","q":"noun"},"stewardships":{"h":"stewardships","l":"stewardship","q":"noun"},"hurdle":{"h":"hurdle","i":"/ˈhɜːrdl/","a":"en-us-hurdle.ogg","s":[{"p":"noun","d":[{"m":"(countable) A hurdle is a difficulty or problem that must be overcome.","e":"Learning a new language can be a big hurdle."},{"m":"(countable) A hurdle is a barrier that runners jump over in a race.","e":"She knocked down a hurdle during the race."}]},{"p":"verb","d":[{"m":"(transitive) If you hurdle a problem, you overcome it successfully.","e":"They managed to hurdle all the challenges in the project."},{"m":"(transitive) If you hurdle something, you jump over it, especially while running.","e":"The athlete hurdled the fence with ease."}]}]},"spur":{"h":"spur","i":"/spɜːr/","a":"en-us-spur.ogg","s":[{"p":"verb","d":[{"m":"(transitive) If you spur someone or something, you encourage or push them to take action.","e":"The teacher's words spurred him to study harder."},{"m":"(transitive) If you spur a horse, you make it go faster by using spurs on your boots.","e":"He spurred his horse i